export const title = "Relay";
export const html = "<h1>Relay</h1>\n      <hr class=\"rule\" />\n\n<p>Relay is a feature that outputs a digital signal through the device's I/O terminals. You can use it to control a warning light or work with a door lock as an entry-control signal, among other uses.</p>\n\n<p>Relay actions can be added from the action settings screen.</p>\n<div class=\"figure\" style=\"max-width:340px;\">\n<div class=\"app-panel\">\n  <div class=\"form-grid\" style=\"grid-template-columns:80px 1fr;\">\n    <span>Action Type</span><select disabled><option>Relay</option></select>\n  </div>\n</div>\n</div>\n\n<p>Setting the action type to Relay reveals the related settings below it.</p>\n<div class=\"figure\" style=\"max-width:360px;\">\n<div class=\"app-panel\">\n  <div class=\"form-grid\" style=\"grid-template-columns:90px 1fr;\">\n    <span>Output Type</span>\n    <select disabled><option>On for Duration</option></select>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"margin-top:8px;font-size:12px;\">\n    <div style=\"display:flex;justify-content:space-between;padding:6px 8px;color:var(--blue);font-weight:700;background:var(--blue-light);border-radius:4px;\">On for Duration <span style=\"color:var(--text-muted);font-weight:400;\">High Priority</span></div>\n    <div style=\"display:flex;justify-content:space-between;padding:6px 8px;\">Off for Duration <span style=\"color:var(--text-muted);\">High Priority</span></div>\n    <div style=\"display:flex;justify-content:space-between;padding:6px 8px;\">ON <span style=\"color:var(--text-muted);\">Normal Priority</span></div>\n    <div style=\"display:flex;justify-content:space-between;padding:6px 8px;\">OFF <span style=\"color:var(--text-muted);\">Normal Priority</span></div>\n  </div>\n</div>\n</div>\n\n<p>The relay's output type is effectively either ON or OFF, but the settings screen presents 4 options to choose from. Each output type is defined as follows.</p>\n\n<div class=\"figure\">\n<table class=\"app-table\">\n<tr><th>Output Type</th><th>Description</th><th>Priority</th></tr>\n<tr><td>On for Duration</td><td>Outputs ON for a set duration.</td><td>High</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Off for Duration</td><td>Outputs OFF for a set duration.</td><td>High</td></tr>\n<tr><td>ON</td><td>Changes the alarm output state to ON.</td><td>Low</td></tr>\n<tr><td>OFF</td><td>Changes the alarm output state to OFF.</td><td>Low</td></tr>\n</table>\n</div>\n\n<p>Notice the priority listed to the right of each output type. Because the number of physical relays is limited, while many event action items can be configured to use them, contention can arise over control of a relay device.</p>\n\n<h2>Relay Control Priority Policy</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>When multiple relay actions of the same priority fire, the item that fired most recently takes control.</li>\n<li>When a high-priority output action competes with a low-priority output action, the high-priority item takes control. Since high-priority alarms have a duration, once that time elapses the relay reverts to the state of whichever low-priority item fired last.</li>\n<li>Low-priority items have no duration, so they permanently change the output's default state until a new request arrives from another event action.</li>\n</ol>";
